1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,290
“The largest empire in history.” That phrase

2
00:00:03,113 --> 00:00:04,597
is not about governments.

3
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:05,905
It is about maps.

4
00:00:06,334 --> 00:00:08,484
The numbers behind the rankings come from

5
00:00:08,295 --> 00:00:09,630
historical atlases:

6
00:00:09,855 --> 00:00:12,119
they measure the coloured patch the atlas

7
00:00:11,930 --> 00:00:13,701
paints for each empire.

8
00:00:14,122 --> 00:00:16,707
That way, the British Empire reaches about

9
00:00:16,519 --> 00:00:20,022
35 million square kilometres around 1920.

10
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:23,114
The Spanish, 13 to 14 million around

11
00:00:22,925 --> 00:00:24,020
1780.

12
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:26,740
But painting a territory is not governing

13
00:00:26,552 --> 00:00:26,935
it.

14
00:00:27,063 --> 00:00:30,027
One colour can hide colonies, protectorates and

15
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:30,887
mere claims.

16
00:00:31,306 --> 00:00:33,696
And the alternative effective control figures going

17
00:00:33,508 --> 00:00:34,178
around?

18
00:00:34,306 --> 00:00:35,561
They don't hold up either:

19
00:00:35,706 --> 00:00:37,500
they don't say which year they measure,

20
00:00:37,368 --> 00:00:38,612
or by what rules.

21
00:00:39,022 --> 00:00:41,103
The useful question is not which one

22
00:00:40,914 --> 00:00:43,178
was bigger, but what each one really

23
00:00:42,990 --> 00:00:44,956
controlled, where and when.

24
00:00:45,370 --> 00:00:47,290
Share it before your next argument about

25
00:00:47,102 --> 00:00:47,898
empires.

